## Setup

With [just](https://just.systems/man/en/packages.html) and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/)
installed (Windows: `winget install Casey.Just` and
`winget install astral-sh.uv`):

```sh
just setup               # rustup, the {{ t.rust_target }} target, {{ setup_tools | join(", ") }}, git hooks
just setup --check       # only report what is missing
```

`scripts/setup.py` runs on Arch, Ubuntu / Debian and Windows and skips what is already there. Before it changes
anything it prints its plan. Some steps fetch a script from the internet and run it, the way these tools are meant
to be installed: rustup's installer (`https://sh.rustup.rs`; on Windows winget installs rustup){% if probe_rs %}, probe-rs's installer
(on Windows its PowerShell installer, run with `irm | iex` and `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass`), and probe-rs's udev rules
file, pinned to a commit, written as root{% endif %}. The plan names each of them before it runs.

Steps that need root or an administrator are printed for you to run, never run behind your back: the system
packages (a C compiler, git, curl; on Windows the MSVC build tools), the udev rule that lets you open the {{ probe[0] }} on
Linux ([probe-rs](https://probe.rs/docs/getting-started/probe-setup/)){{ " and ST's ST-LINK driver on Windows" if windows_driver else "" }}.
`just setup --root` runs them too, each shown first; `--no-probe` leaves out the probe's udev rule or driver (a build
server), `--skip TOOL` leaves out one step.

The last step turns on the project's git hooks (`just hooks`, see [Changing the API](#changing-the-api)): for each
hook in `.githooks/` it puts a small hook into `.git/hooks/` that first runs the hook that was there before (kept as
`<name>.chained`) and then the tracked one, so hooks you or other tools installed keep working. It does not set
`core.hooksPath`; when that is set already, it prints the line to add to the hooks there. `just hooks --remove` takes
them out again and puts the old hooks back.

## Run

```sh
just run                 # build, flash and run the dev firmware with its defmt log (Ctrl-C: the firmware keeps running)
just cnt                 # same, but through `cnt run`: defmt log plus the event counters as they change
just flash               # release firmware through the probe
{% if s.has_cli %}
just install             # build and install the `{{ s.cli }}` command line
{{ (s.cli ~ ' info').ljust(24) }} # API, firmware, build id, board, chip, label, last reset
{% if o.led %}
{{ s.cli }} led blink -n 5
{% endif %}
{% if o.settings_flash %}
{{ (s.cli ~ ' label "bench 1"').ljust(24) }} # stored in the board's flash
{% endif %}
{% if o.crash %}
{{ s.cli }} last-reset --clear
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
```

The recipes and `cargo run` use only {{ s.probe_name }} (USB id `{{ s.probe }}`), never another probe on the
PC; with two {{ R.get("plural", "boards") }} attached, `{{ s.probe_env }}=SERIAL` (from `probe-rs list`, in the environment or in a `.env` file)
picks one.{% if s.has_cli %} A probe can be opened by one program at a time: stop `just run` / `just attach` before using `{{ s.cli }}`.{% if ww.rtt %} Connecting
scans the RAM for the RTT control block (about 1.5 s through an {{ probe[0] }}); `--elf firmware/target/{{ t.rust_target }}/debug/{{ o.name }}`
(or `{{ s.elf_env }}`) takes the address from the firmware's ELF instead.{% endif %} With several probes attached, `--probe SERIAL`
(`{{ s.cli }} probes` lists them, and the shell completes the serials: `source <(COMPLETE=bash {{ s.cli }})`).{% endif +%}

`cnt tui --chip {{ s.chip }}` shows the event counters; `ww` (the WireWeaver CLI,
`cargo install --git https://github.com/vhrdtech/wire_weaver wire_weaver_cli`) can introspect and call any API
without the generated client: `ww {{ "--rtt " ~ s.chip ~ " " if ww.rtt else "" }}introspect`.
